# The two committed build artifacts must CONFLICT, never auto-merge.
#
# dist/index.html and dist/artifact.html are tracked on purpose — they are the
# thing you double-click, and the booth machine has no npm and no network. But
# they are GENERATED: build.js inlines every src/ module verbatim into a script
# inside one document, so a single line of source lands in both files, far from
# where it was written and with no import graph left to make the relationship
# visible.
#
# Git cannot see any of that. It sees two ~1 MB text files with long common
# runs, and its default line merge is only too happy to interleave hunks from
# two branches that each rebuilt from a different tree. During the eight-branch
# rebrand integration it did exactly that, twice, with no conflict raised. The
# result happened to be correct — and that is the problem, because nothing about
# the process made it correct and the next integrator gets no warning at all.
# A generated file merged line-by-line is a build nobody ran.
#
# merge=binary is git's built-in "take ours and declare a conflict" driver. It
# turns a silent, plausible, unbuilt artifact into a loud stop. There is exactly
# one correct resolution and it is never to hand-edit the conflict:
#
#     git checkout --ours dist/index.html dist/artifact.html
#     node build.js --3d --artifact        # rebuild from the merged source
#     git add dist/index.html dist/artifact.html
#
# diff is deliberately left ON. Reading the diff of a rebuilt artifact is how
# you catch a source change that did not survive inlining — the "correct in
# source, broken in the artifact" failure this repo keeps producing.
dist/*.html merge=binary

# assets/avatar.glb and the vendor bundle are genuinely binary / generated too;
# marking them keeps a merge from ever trying to be clever with them either.
assets/*.glb          binary
vendor/*.bundle.js    merge=binary
